For one time period, the waveform is \[V(t)=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{2t}{T}, &amp; 0\le t\le \dfrac{T}{2}\\ 0, &amp; \dfrac{T}{2}&lt;t\le T
\end{cases}\] The RMS value is \[V_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\int_{0}^{T}V^2(t)\,dt}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\int_{0}^{T/2}\left(\frac{2t}{T}\right)^2dt}.\] Evaluating the integral, \[V_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{4}{T^3}\left[\frac{t^3}{3}\right]_{0}^{T/2}}
=\sqrt{\frac{4}{T^3}\cdot\frac{T^3}{24}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{6}}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }\sqrt{\frac{1}{6}}}\]

Using the trigonometric identity, \[\sin^2 x=\frac{1-\cos 2x}{2}.\] Substituting the identity directly, \[f(x)=\sin^2x
=\frac{1-\cos2x}{2}
=\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{2}\cos2x.\] This is already the Fourier series expansion of the given function. \[\boxed{\text{(d) }0.5-0.5\cos2x}\]

Given, \[F(z)=\frac{1}{z+1}
=\frac{z^{-1}}{1+z^{-1}}
=\frac{z^{-1}}{1-(-1)z^{-1}}.\] Using the standard pair, \[\frac{z^{-1}}{1-az^{-1}}
\overset{\mathcal{Z}}{\longleftrightarrow}
a^{n-1}u[n-1],\] with \[a=-1,\] we obtain \[f[k]=(-1)^{k-1}u[k-1].\] Since \[u[k-1]=u[k]-\delta[k],\] we get \[\begin{aligned}
f[k]
&amp;=(-1)^{k-1}(u[k]-\delta[k])\\
&amp;=-(-1)^ku[k]+\delta[k].
\end{aligned}\] For \(k\ge0\), \[u[k]=1.\] Hence, \[f[k]=\delta[k]-(-1)^k.\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }\delta(k)-(-1)^k.}\]

Trans-conductance of MOSFET is given by \[\begin{aligned}
g_m &amp;= \frac{\partial i_D}{\partial V_{GS}} \\ &amp;= \frac{(2 - 1)\,\text{mA}}{(2 - 1)\,\text{V}} = 1\,\text{mS}
\end{aligned}\] Hence (B) is correct option. \[\boxed{\textbf{B. } \text{1 ms}}\]

\[G(s)=\frac{s+1}{s^2}\] For a unity-feedback system, the gain margin is determined at the phase crossover frequency where \[\angle G(j\omega)=-180^\circ.\] Substituting \(s=j\omega\), \[G(j\omega)
=\frac{1+j\omega}{(j\omega)^2}
=-\frac{1+j\omega}{\omega^2}.\] Hence, \[\angle G(j\omega)
=-180^\circ+\tan^{-1}(\omega).\] For every finite \(\omega&gt;0\), \[-180^\circ&lt;\angle G(j\omega)&lt;-90^\circ.\] The phase approaches \(-180^\circ\) only as \[\omega\rightarrow0.\] At this frequency, \[|G(j\omega)|
=\frac{\sqrt{1+\omega^2}}{\omega^2}
\rightarrow\infty.\] Therefore, there is no finite phase-crossover frequency and the gain margin is \[\boxed{\mathrm{GM}=\infty}.\] \[\boxed{\text{Answer: (D) }\infty}\]

Given, \[G(s)H(s)=\frac{\pi e^{-0.25s}}{s}\] Put \(s=j\omega\), \[G(j\omega)H(j\omega)
=
\frac{\pi e^{-j0.25\omega}}{j\omega}
=
\frac{\pi}{\omega}
e^{-j\left(90^\circ+\dfrac{\omega}{4}\right)}\] Therefore, \[\left|G(j\omega)H(j\omega)\right|
=
\frac{\pi}{\omega}\] \[\angle G(j\omega)H(j\omega)
=
-90^\circ-\frac{\omega}{4}\] The Nyquist plot crosses the negative real axis when \[\angle G(j\omega)H(j\omega)
=
-180^\circ\] \[-90^\circ-\frac{\omega}{4}
=
-180^\circ\] \[\frac{\omega}{4}
=
\frac{\pi}{2}\] \[\omega=2\pi\] At \(\omega=2\pi\), \[\left|G(j\omega)H(j\omega)\right|
=
\frac{\pi}{2\pi}
=
\frac{1}{2}
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
\angle G(j\omega)H(j\omega)
=
-90^\circ-\frac{2\pi}{4}
=-\frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{\pi}{2}
=-\pi\] Hence, \[G(j\omega)H(j\omega)=\frac{1}{2}~\angle-\pi=-\frac{1}{2}\] Therefore, the Nyquist plot passes through \(\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},\,j0\right)\) \[\boxed{(B)\;\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},\,j0\right)}\]

