For an induction motor, \[P_{ag}=\text{Air-gap power}\] Rotor copper loss is \[P_{cu}=sP_{ag}\] Hence, gross mechanical power developed (gross power output) is \[P_{g}
=
P_{ag}-P_{cu}\] \[P_{g}
=
P_{ag}-sP_{ag}\] \[P_{g}
=
(1-s)P_{ag}\] Therefore, \[\frac{P_{g}}{P_{ag}}
=
1-s\] Hence, \[\boxed{B}\]

Developed torque in an induction motor is \[T=\frac{3}{\omega_{s}}\,I_{2}^{\prime\,2}\,\frac{R_{2}^{\prime}}{s}\] Hence, the ratio of starting torque to full-load torque is \[\frac{(T)_{\text{starting}}}{(T)_{\text{full-load}}}
=
\left(\frac{I_{\text{st}}}{I_{\text{fl}}}\right)^2 s_{\text{fl}}\] In the above expression, rotor current is replaced by stator current since the ratio of rotor and stator currents remains the same. Given, \[\frac{I_{\text{st}}}{I_{\text{fl}}}=6\] and \[s_{\text{fl}}=0.04\] Therefore, \[\frac{(T)_{\text{starting}}}{(T)_{\text{full-load}}}
=
6^2 \times 0.04\] \[\frac{(T)_{\text{starting}}}{(T)_{\text{full-load}}}
=
36 \times 0.04
=
1.44\] Hence, \[(T)_{\text{starting}}=1.44\,(T)_{\text{full-load}}\] \[\boxed{B}\]

In salient-pole synchronous machines, the air gap along the \(d\)-axis is smaller than that along the \(q\)-axis. Therefore, reluctance along the \(d\)-axis is less, which results in \[X_{d} &gt; X_{q}\] A synchronous motor is not self-starting because the rotor cannot continuously follow the rotating magnetic field at synchronous speed. The average starting torque is zero. Damper bars are provided to produce induction motor action during starting and help the machine accelerate close to synchronous speed. The short-circuit ratio (SCR) is defined as \[\text{SCR}
=
\frac{I_{f}\,(\text{rated }V_{oc})}
{I_{f}\,(\text{rated }I_{sc})}\] and is the reciprocal of per-unit synchronous reactance. The V-curve of a synchronous motor represents the variation of armature current with field excitation at constant output power. Hence, the third statement is false. Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{\text{C}}\]

Given, \[S=1000\,\text{kVA},\qquad V_{L}=6.6\,\text{kV},\qquad X_{s}=20\,\Omega\] At full load and unity power factor, \[I_{a}
=
\frac{S}{\sqrt{3}V_{L}}\] \[I_{a}
=
\frac{1000\times10^{3}}{\sqrt{3}\times6600}
=
87.48\,\text{A}\] Phase voltage is \[V_{ph}
=
\frac{6600}{\sqrt{3}}
=
3810.5\,\text{V}\] For a synchronous generator, \[\overline{E}_{ph}
=
\overline{V}_{ph}
+
jI_{a}X_{s}\] \[E_{ph}
=
\sqrt{V_{ph}^{2}+\left(I_{a}X_{s}\right)^{2}}\] \[E_{ph}
=
\sqrt{(3810.5)^2+(87.48\times20)^2}
=
4193\,\text{V}\] Line-to-line induced emf is \[E_{L}
=
\sqrt{3}E_{ph}\] \[E_{L}
=
\sqrt{3}\times4193
=
7262\,\text{V}
\approx
7.2\,\text{kV}\] Therefore, \[\boxed{\text{B}}\]

In a single-phase induction motor, the total developed torque is the difference between the forward-field torque and the backward-field torque. \[T_{\text{total}}=T_{f}-T_{b}\] where, \[T_{f}=\text{Torque due to forward field}\] \[T_{b}=\text{Torque due to backward field}\] Hence, \[T_{\text{total}}
=
\frac{I_{2}^{\prime\,2}R_{2}^{\prime}}{2\omega_{s}}
\left(
\frac{1}{s}
-
\frac{1}{2-s}
\right)\] Thus, \[T_{\text{total}}\propto R_{2}^{\prime}\] Therefore, increasing the rotor resistance increases the total starting torque. A higher starting torque produces a larger accelerating torque, enabling the motor to reach its operating speed more quickly. Hence, a high-resistance rotor is used to obtain quick acceleration. \[\boxed{B}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>In a single-phase induction motor, total torque is the difference between forward and backward field torques.</li><li>Rotor resistance increases the starting torque.</li><li>Higher starting torque results in higher accelerating torque.</li><li>High-resistance rotors are preferred where quick acceleration is required.</li></ul></div>

