Maximum value of input line voltage is \[V_m=400\sqrt{2}\,\text{V}\] If \(V_{AB}\) is taken as reference, then for diode bridge rectifier, \[V_o=V_{AB}
\qquad
\text{for } 60^\circ \leq \omega t \leq 120^\circ\] Since, \[V_{AB}=400\sqrt{2}\sin\omega t\] Hence, maximum output voltage occurs at \[\omega t=90^\circ\] Therefore, peak instantaneous output voltage is \[V_m=400\sqrt{2}\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{\text{B}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>In a three-phase diode bridge rectifier, the output voltage is equal to the maximum instantaneous line-to-line voltage.</li></ul></div>

The Fourier series of the line voltage of a three-phase square-wave inverter operating in \(180^\circ\) conduction mode is \[V_{ab}
=
\sum_{n=1,3,5,\ldots}^{\infty}
\frac{4V_s}{n\pi}
\cos\left(\frac{n\pi}{6}\right)
\sin\left(n\omega t+\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\] For triplen harmonics, \[n=3,9,15,\ldots\] Since, \[\cos\left(\frac{3\pi}{6}\right)
=
\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)
=
0,\] all triplen harmonics are absent from the line voltage. The line-to-neutral (phase) voltage in \(180^\circ\) conduction mode is \[V_{ao}
=
\sum_{n=6k\pm1}^{\infty}
\frac{2V_s}{n\pi}
\sin(n\omega t),\] where \[k=0,1,2,\ldots\] Thus, the phase voltage contains only harmonics of the order \[n=6k\pm1,\] which are all odd harmonics. Similarly, for \(120^\circ\) conduction mode, \[V_{ao}
=
\sum_{n=1,3,5,\ldots}^{\infty}
\frac{2V_s}{n\pi}
\cos\left(\frac{n\pi}{6}\right)
\sin\left(n\omega t+\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\] and the line voltage is \[V_{ab}
=
\sum_{n=6k\pm1}^{\infty}
\frac{3V_s}{n\pi}
\sin\left(n\omega t+\frac{\pi}{3}\right),\] where \[k=0,1,2,\ldots\] Hence, in both \(180^\circ\) and \(120^\circ\) conduction modes, the output voltage waveform contains only odd harmonics. Therefore, the correct answer is \[\boxed{(C)\ \text{Only odd harmonics}}\]

Given, \[x(t)=P\sin(4t+30^\circ)\] So the angular frequency of X-input is \[\omega_x=4\,\text{rad/s}\] For a stationary Lissajous figure, \[\frac{f_x}{f_y}=\frac{\text{number of tangencies on vertical sides}}{\text{number of tangencies on horizontal sides}}\] <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> From the given figure-8 pattern, \[\frac{f_x}{f_y}=\frac{4}{2}\] Hence, \[f_y=\frac{f_x}{2}
\Rightarrow
\omega_y=\frac{\omega_x}{2}
=\frac{4}{2}
=2\,\text{rad/s}\] So the Y-input must have angular frequency \(2\,\text{rad/s}\), and from the given options the matching signal is: \[y(t)=Q\sin(2t+15^\circ)\] \[\boxed{\textbf{B. }Q\sin(2t+15^\circ)}\]
